The container needs a measurable width for the component to determine whether the text wraps.

## Evaluation

Assistant B is closer to a usable answer: it provides a component, an example of use, and a way to style the text. However, its claim that the code detects overflow is unreliable: it checks `onTextLayout` on a `<Text>` already limited to one line, so the reported lines may not reveal that the original text wrapped. Also, it appends “See more” after the full text inside that same line-limited `<Text>`. For long text, the appended control can itself be truncated and never appear. These issues undermine the main requested behavior, despite the overall structure being relevant.

Assistant A has more serious problems. `contentRef` is never declared, and `contentHeight` is assigned without being declared or used meaningfully. `layoutSize` is not a standard React Native ref measurement property, `ellipsis` is not the relevant `<Text>` prop, and `lineWidth` is not a valid text style property. It also sets the collapsed text to five lines rather than one, shows the “See More” control unconditionally, and does not actually reveal all text when expanded.

Neither answer is production-ready as presented. B is still substantially more helpful than A because it has a coherent component structure and usage example, even though its overflow detection and inline control placement need correction. The main missing detail in both answers is reliable measurement of the full, unconstrained text and ensuring the “See more” control remains visible when the text is truncated.

My final verdict is Assistant B is significantly better: [[B>>A]]
